  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 AM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nThe well-advertised upper-level ridge remains the primary story this \nmorning, bringing heat and humidity and a non-zero chance of a stray \nthunderstorm. Water vapor imagery shows the ridge axis cresting \nnorthern Illinois and even far southern Michigan. Upstream \nconvection over northern IA and southeast MN has been rather cyclic \nin its intensity on IR Satellite, but the eastern extend of activity \nis expanding across central-southern Lake Michigan. Given the ridge \naxis placement, high confidence in dry conditions this morning. \nHowever, while I've shipped out a dry forecast today (10% PoP), I \ncannot completely rule out a stray thunderstorms this afternoon. The \nprimary forcing mechanism could be a departing 500-mb jet streak, \nbut this focuses primary across central MI and into Ontario. \nMeanwhile, a second group of CAMs suggests renewed, isoalted activity \nalong the IL-IN border in the 4p-6p ET window. This might be forced \nby a lingering outflow/MCV from the aformentioned upstream activity. \nWill pass this on to the incoming shift for further consideration. \n\nOtherwise, very hot and humid once again today with highs in the low-\n90s, dew points well into the 70s resulting in heat indicies of 100-\n105 (as seen yesterday). \n\nOn Thursday, the 500mb ridge folds somewhat in response to a \ndisturbance wrapping up over interior Ontario. The result locally is \nan increasing chance for showers and thunderstorms as the day \nprogresses. Timing is uncertain, with some guidance favoring the \novernight period instead of the late-afternoon. Our hot and humid \nenvironment will be supportive of isolated severe thunderstorms, \nbut this risk is highly conditional on how any upstream activity \nevolves. These storms also decrease forecast confidence in the \ntemperature forecast for Thursday. Due to anticipated cloud debris, \nwe'll hold off on any extension of the heat advisory.\n\nWith the upper-level ridge somewhat displaced, we could remain \nwithin an active period into at least the start of the Labor Day \nweekend with showers and storms around both Friday and Saturday. \nBeyond this, there is some indication the ridge may wash back north \nearly next week. High temperatures Monday and Tuesday have a \ncomically large spread (10+ degrees Tuesday) among the guidance, \nwith the in-house blend on the cool-side. With that much spread, \nprecipitation chances are likewise unclear. Given the trough working \nits way across the Cascades and through Montana, I would favor the \nridge axis to revisit Indiana early in the week. No signs of fall \nquite yet.",
